<title>USA: CME LAUDS &quot;SHORT-SHORT&quot; RULE REPEAL IN US TAX DEAL.</title>
<headline>CME LAUDS &quot;SHORT-SHORT&quot; RULE REPEAL IN US TAX DEAL.</headline>
<dateline>CHICAGO 1997-08-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Chicago Mercantile Exchange (CME) on Wednesday applauded the repeal of the so-called &quot;short-short&quot; rule in the U.S. budget package signed into law by President Bill Clinton earlier this week.</p>
<p>The repeal is seen allowing expanded use of derivatives by mutual funds which had been limited by tax implications.</p>
<p>&quot;The onerous tax burden on investment held less than 90 days effectively banished millions of mutual fund investors from the economic benefits of risk management tools such as futures and options,&quot; CME chairman emeritus Leo Melamed said in a statement.</p>
</text>
